---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CardDetail from '../components/CardDetail.astro';
import FamilyLine from '../components/FamilyLine.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import { CURRENT, EDGE_FEATURES, STATUS, product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('edge');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Edge: store-and-forward and an OPC UA server"
  description={`HotLoop Edge is IoT plus the machine layer: store-and-forward that survives a dead WAN, and an OPC UA server for your SCADA. ${CURRENT} is out.`}
  ogAlt="HotLoop Edge, IoT plus the machine layer."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/edge/',
      description:
        'HotLoop IoT plus the machine layer: store-and-forward over Sparkplug B that survives a dead uplink, and an OPC UA server, for a box that sits next to a machine.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      softwareVersion: CURRENT,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Edge</span><span class="chip ok">{CURRENT} is out</span></div>
      <h1>IoT, plus everything that lives next to a machine.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A machine's screen and logic should not depend on a network three buildings away. Edge is HotLoop on a box next to
        the machine: everything in <a href="/iot/">IoT</a>, plus an OPC UA server your SCADA can read and a queue that
        holds readings and replays them in order when the uplink comes back. When the WAN dies, the operator still has a
        screen, the alarm still raises, and the rule still runs. A cut fiber stays an IT ticket, and the line keeps
        running.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>HotLoop Edge {CURRENT} is out.</strong>
          <p>
            It ships beside IoT, the Gateway, and the Edge Relay from the same release, every time. It runs on PCs,
            servers, and Raspberry Pis, including the 32-bit ones, it is about 34 MB on a Pi, and it installs with no
            login and no license key. If the box only has to poll and forward, the <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a>
            is the smaller one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://docs.hotloop.io/edge/overview/">Docs</a>
      </div>
      <FamilyLine id="edge" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">What it does</span>
        <h2>A box at the machine that keeps working when the network doesn't.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          The operator's screen runs against the box on the machine, not a server three buildings away. When the
          uplink drops, readings are held and replayed in order, flagged historical, when it comes back. Pull the power
          with the link up and you lose what was in memory, about one poll, and the docs say so. Your historian gets a
          filled-in gap instead of a hole.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Where it fits</span>
        <h2>In the middle of the lineup, and not a dead end.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          <a href="/iot/">IoT</a> is the base, and Edge is IoT plus the machine layer. The <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a>
          is Edge plus fleet, multi-site and reports, and the <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> is the one with no screen.
          One codebase, one release. The database is a setting: <strong>{p.databases}</strong>, with
          TimescaleDB (PostgreSQL built for time-series data) when the box keeps real history and SQLite on a small PC with a
          small disk. Moving up to the Gateway keeps your data and your config.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What Edge adds to IoT</span>
        <h2>What Edge adds, and where each piece stands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          "{STATUS.released.label}" means it ships in the published release.
          "{STATUS.planned.label}" means exactly that. Everything in <a href="/iot/">IoT</a> comes along too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <FeatureList features={EDGE_FEATURES} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">HMI (operator screens), what is built and what is the plan</span>
        <h2>One UI, not two.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          HMI screens aren't a second front end bolted on the side. They're the dashboards IoT gets, bound to a
          physical screen, and that part is out in every product with a UI. The symbol library and faceplates below are still ahead. They use the same
          bindings and the same write gate, so there is one interface to learn and one set of permission checks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Built, and out in every product</h3>
          <p class="muted">Dashboards, bound to a physical screen, and panel mode: the dashboard bound to a screen fills the panel, with no header, big touch targets, and it works with the uplink down.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Planned, and not built</h3>
          <p class="muted">The ISA-101 symbol library, motor and valve faceplates, and PIN sign-in for shared panels, so the audit names a person and not "panel 3". They build on the dashboards and the same write gate, and this page will say when they ship.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <CardDetail label="The planned HMI work, in detail">
        <div><h4>A canvas that never reflows</h4><p>A fixed screen that scales and never shuffles things around, because an operator's thumb goes to where the pump was yesterday, and it had better still be there.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Faceplates</h4><p>Click a motor and get its popup: start, stop, reset, mode, speed, its interlocks, and its alarms, built from the motor entity that already exists.</p></div>
        <div><h4>ISA-101 by default</h4><p>The ISA-101 standard for plant screens: gray backgrounds, color only for abnormal, alarm priority by shape as well as color. When the whole screen is gray, the one red thing gets seen. No 3D gradients on the tank.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Panel mode</h4><p>The dashboard bound to a screen fills the panel with no header, no tabs and big touch targets. It works against the box itself with the uplink down, so the alarm list on a panel is an alarms card on its dashboard.</p></div>
        <div><h4>A person behind every write</h4><p>On your own computer you're already signed in. A shared panel signs operators in with a PIN or a badge and signs them out when idle, so the audit names a human, not "panel 3".</p></div>
      </CardDetail>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>On a cluster, or on a plain Linux box.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          One chart repository holds all four charts, and a box can move up to the Gateway later without losing a thing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>On Kubernetes</h3>
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install hotloop hotloop/hotloop-edge --version {CURRENT} \
  --namespace hotloop --create-namespace</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            The chart runs TimescaleDB by default and keeps 90 days of history. PostgreSQL and SQLite are one setting
            away. Store-and-forward comes with the chart, and its queue is capped so a long outage can't fill the disk.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>On a plain box</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            For a box outside any cluster, plain container service files start Edge at boot with nobody logged in. The
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/install/quadlet/">install guide</a> has them.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            The app follows the stable release and rolls back an update that can't start.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Tested</span>
        <h2>Run on real hardware, so you don't find out at commissioning.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Starts at boot, updates itself</h3><p>The service files that run Edge on a plain Linux box were tested on bare metal (2026-11-17).</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Small Raspberry Pis</h3><p>The 32-bit ARM build is published and was tested on bare metal (2026-11-17).</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Moving up a product</h3><p>IoT to Edge to the Gateway on one database has been run on a plain Linux box, and each step kept the data and the config.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="notice info after">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>S7comm and EtherNet/IP have run on bare metal (2026-11-17).</strong>
          <p>
            As with any new controller, confirm a value you already know at commissioning. Each driver's tests are on
            <a href="/integrations/">the integrations page</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            Edge is under the HotLoop Source License Agreement 1.0, like the rest of the lineup. It is free to use and source-available,
not OSI open source. Home and lab use is free. Business use is free through <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNet</a>. Edge isn't a paid tier of
            anything, because there is no paid tier to be one of.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .after { margin-top: var(--hl-s6); }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
